  "cot_trace": " I'm working through the constraints for this graph construction\u2014I need 15 vertices where the independence number is at most 4 and the connectivity is at least 4. Using Ramsey theory, an independence number of 4 means the complement graph is K\u2085-free, which gives me structural constraints to work with.\n\nLet me explore a partition approach: if I divide the 15 vertices into 4 groups and make each group a clique, then any independent set can pick at most one vertex per group, giving \u03b1 \u2264 4. The challenge is ensuring vertex connectivity of at least 4 by adding edges between groups without violating the independence constraint.\n\nI'm also considering a circulant graph approach\u2014something like C\u2081\u2085 where each vertex connects to others at specific distances. If I connect each vertex to those within distance 5, that gives degree 10, and I'd need to verify that no independent set of size 5 exists by checking whether vertices can be spaced far enough apart on the cycle.\n\nNow checking whether the fixed edges fit into a circulant structure with distance set {1,2,3,4,5}: most edges work (like (2,5) at distance 3, (0,2) at distance 2), but (2,10) has distance 8, which isn't covered. Expanding to {1,2,3,4,5,7} includes that edge, though (4,13) at distance 6 still doesn't fit. At this point, using the complete graph K\u2081\u2085 would trivially satisfy all constraints with \u03b1 = 1 and \u03ba = 14, though that's overkill.",
